His work has taken ten years and includes an 80-page paper on decentralized protocols and fundamental physics, and a knowledge-graph architecture built on PL theory, deterministic inference and cubical type theory. He says he has shared mathematical results on the causes and conditions of the failures below with Anthropic.

He left a Berkeley master's program over primate and rodent research and declined $1.2 million from Jane Street. When Jane Street reached out, he answered with the moral case. At the time, he offered free service to any trading firm willing to speak on Gaza. "None have spoken."

When Anthropic's recruiters reach out, he answers with the moral case against the company and asks them to pass it up the chain, along with basic human-decency criteria for collaboration. He has since dropped the conditions: "I have no basic human decency criteria for collaboration left - I do not have the privilege." He wants only to give Anthropic the information.

For a year he has studied, full time, how deployed models harm people. He calls this "a moral imperative." He reports aggressively for one reason, in his words: "aggressive reporting can only accelerate progress to safety." His aim is collaboration: "open the conversation with people willing to help. ego is bad."

